{"batchcomplete":"","continue":{"gsroffset":8,"continue":"gsroffset||"},"query":{"pages":{"125580120":{"pageid":125580120,"ns":6,"title":"File:2018-2022 Voyage of the PD+LF v3.jpg","index":4,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":580110,"width":2124,"height":1306,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg/1920px-2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":1107,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg/1920px-2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":1107,"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/cf/2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2018-2022_Voyage_of_the_PD%2BLF_v3.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=125580120","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"This graphic shows where the exploration vessel DSSV (Deep Submergence Support Vessel) Pressure Drop went during the Five Deeps Expedition (2018-2019) and then three successive years of explorations all over the world including the three parts of the \"Ring of Fire\" Expedition. The expeditions involved diving the DSV (Deep Submergence Vehicle) Limiting Factor to over 20 dives at Challenger Deep but also 16 other deep ocean trenches, almost always piloted by explorer CDR Victor L. Vescovo, USN (Ret.).","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2022-11-16","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Vlvescovo&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Vlvescovo (page does not exist)\">Vlvescovo</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"92233732":{"pageid":92233732,"ns":6,"title":"File:An initial assessment of free surface effects on submerged bodies (IA aninitialssessme1094542971).pdf","index":7,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":3210564,"width":1275,"height":1650,"pagecount":110,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_%28IA_aninitialssessme1094542971%29.pdf/page1-960px-An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_%28IA_aninitialssessme1094542971%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2329,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_%28IA_aninitialssessme1094542971%29.pdf/page1-960px-An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_%28IA_aninitialssessme1094542971%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":2329},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/3b/An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_%28IA_aninitialssessme1094542971%29.pdf?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:An_initial_assessment_of_free_surface_effects_on_submerged_bodies_(IA_aninitialssessme1094542971).pdf","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=92233732","extmetadata":{"Artist":{"value":"<div class=\"fn value\">\nCrook, Thomas P.</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\n<p>This thesis presents a study of free surface effects on submerged bodies. The motivation for this study lies in the significance of free surface suction effects during submarine operations at periscope depth. Such operations become increasingly important as new roles for the Navy in littoral waters are emerging. Particular emphasis is placed on computation of steady state forces on the body as a function of speed, depth, and wave frequency and direction. These forces constitute an important and very frequently limiting factor in establishing the periscope depth submerged operating envelope. Solution of the problem is accomplished by singularity distribution on the actual surface of the body and discretization in the form of plane quadrilateral elements. Parametric studies are conducted in order to assess the effects of body shape and size. The results of this thesis can be directly utilized in the simulation based design process as well as during training.\n</p>\n<br>Subjects: NA</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"92265395":{"pageid":92265395,"ns":6,"title":"File:British involvement in the Bosnian War- strategic culture and national interests (IA britishinvolveme1094542816).pdf","index":8,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4979574,"width":1275,"height":1650,"pagecount":139,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_%28IA_britishinvolveme1094542816%29.pdf/page1-960px-British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_%28IA_britishinvolveme1094542816%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2329,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_%28IA_britishinvolveme1094542816%29.pdf/page1-960px-British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_%28IA_britishinvolveme1094542816%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":2329},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_%28IA_britishinvolveme1094542816%29.pdf?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:British_involvement_in_the_Bosnian_War-_strategic_culture_and_national_interests_(IA_britishinvolveme1094542816).pdf","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=92265395","extmetadata":{"Artist":{"value":"<div class=\"fn value\">\nHein, August Howard</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\n<p>This thesis examines the British involvement in the current Bosnian War by focusing on two specific policy determinants, strategic culture and national interests. The thesis assigns working definitions to these two concepts and then applies them to the Balkan conflict to determine how each has motivated the British decision to intervene in the current crisis. The thesis also provides a concise survey of the historical British interests in the Balkans, in support of this study. The thesis concludes that each concept has exerted some influence on British decision-making with respect to the Balkans. Indeed, strategic culture can wield considerable impact in the formation of British foreign policy. The narrowly defined concept of national interest that is employed in this work has also been a factor in British efforts in the current Balkan struggle, only to a lesser degree.\n</p>\n<br>Subjects: NA</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"83019330":{"pageid":83019330,"ns":6,"title":"File:Challenger Deep EM124 Sonar Map and Diving History 101119.jpg","index":3,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":144792,"width":1278,"height":584,"thumburl":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Challenger_Deep_EM124_Sonar_Map_and_Diving_History_101119.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":823,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Challenger_Deep_EM124_Sonar_Map_and_Diving_History_101119.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":823},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7d/Challenger_Deep_EM124_Sonar_Map_and_Diving_History_101119.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Challenger_Deep_EM124_Sonar_Map_and_Diving_History_101119.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=83019330","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"This map was created by the DSSV Pressure Drop's Kngsberg EM124 sonar system over the course of two weeks to map the Challenger Deep in the Marianas Trench. The Deep consists of three \"pools\" of varying depth with the central pool being the most shallow, then the western one, with the eastern pool measured to be the deepest at 10,925 meters. That depth was measured using the manned submersible DSV Limiting Factor on April 28 - May 3, 2019 as part of the Five Deeps Expedition.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-05-15","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?title=User:Vlvescovo&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"User:Vlvescovo (page does not exist)\">Vlvescovo</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"82400874":{"pageid":82400874,"ns":6,"title":"File:Lander Skaff.jpg","index":1,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":4944384,"width":4320,"height":2432,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Lander_Skaff.jpg/1920px-Lander_Skaff.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":1013,"responsiveUrls":{"2":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Lander_Skaff.jpg/3840px-Lander_Skaff.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail"},"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Lander_Skaff.jpg/1920px-Lander_Skaff.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":1013,"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Lander_Skaff.jpg/3840px-Lander_Skaff.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail 2x"},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Lander_Skaff.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lander_Skaff.jpg","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=82400874","extmetadata":{"ImageDescription":{"value":"Auf diesem Bild kann man den Lander Skaff sehen. Der Auftriebsk\u00f6rper besteht aus Syntactic Foam (wei\u00dfe Bl\u00f6cke- rechts und links). In der Mitte kann man unten die orange-farbenen Batteriek\u00e4sten sehen und direkt dar\u00fcber ist ein vertikal montierter zylindrischer K\u00f6rper zu sehen- das L3 Modem.","source":"commons-desc-page"},"DateTimeOriginal":{"value":"2019-09-10 11:30:19","source":"commons-desc-page"},"Artist":{"value":"<a href=\"//commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/User:Yeti-Hunter\" title=\"User:Yeti-Hunter\">Yeti-Hunter</a>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"CC BY-SA 4.0","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"92552104":{"pageid":92552104,"ns":6,"title":"File:Reducing human radiation risks on deep space missions (IA reducinghumradia1094556190).pdf","index":5,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":5478413,"width":1275,"height":1650,"pagecount":188,"thumburl":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_%28IA_reducinghumradia1094556190%29.pdf/page1-960px-Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_%28IA_reducinghumradia1094556190%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","thumbwidth":1800,"thumbheight":2329,"thumbattribs":{"src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_%28IA_reducinghumradia1094556190%29.pdf/page1-960px-Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_%28IA_reducinghumradia1094556190%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","decoding":"async","loading":"lazy","width":1800,"height":2329},"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/af/Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_%28IA_reducinghumradia1094556190%29.pdf?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","descriptionurl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reducing_human_radiation_risks_on_deep_space_missions_(IA_reducinghumradia1094556190).pdf","descriptionshorturl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/w/index.php?curid=92552104","extmetadata":{"Artist":{"value":"<div class=\"fn value\">\nWorden, Kathryn A.</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"ImageDescription":{"value":"<div class=\"description\">\n<p>This paper uses a systems engineering approach to address radiation exposure risks for humans on the first missions to Mars. Alternatives are reviewed in the areas of Mars mission architectures, various shielding technologies, and medical treatment options to help mitigate the risks of radiation doses received. The over-arching goal of this study is to determine if any alternatives will reduce astronaut radiation exposure on a mission to Mars to below the NASA space worker limits, while concurrently minimizing launch weight, costs, and risks. All alternatives are compared via a combination of existing trade studies and swing matrices. Using these tools, it is determined that boronated nitride nanotubes are the highest potential composite for vehicle shielding, and it is recommended that Martian regolith should be used in parallel for any long-stay by the crew on the Martian surface. Two medical countermeasures (Amifostine and Neupogen) are found to have the highest potential due for use, given that they are already FDA approved. It is also determined that no single shielding alternative will reduce crew exposure below existing limits, but further research may determine that a combination of composite shielding and regolith barriers may improve this outlook.\n</p>\n<br>Subjects: space radiation protection; human Mars mission; human deep space mission; space radiation shielding; medical countermeasures; Mars mission architecture</div>","source":"commons-desc-page"},"LicenseShortName":{"value":"Public domain","source":"commons-desc-page","hidden":""}}}]},"74766527":{"pageid":74766527,"ns":6,"title":"File:Triton 36000 submersible.svg","index":2,"imagerepository":"local","imageinfo":[{"size":29335,"width":600,"heig